00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.990 align:start position:0%
 
offshore has announced the new price cap  will go up to  3459 pounds  national energy action predicts that  will put 8.2 million people in fuel  poverty from october  it's a huge jump the price cap was  1277 pounds last winter and is currently  set at 1  971 pounds  so let's get more on this i'm joined now  by off gems chief executive jonathan  brielly a very good morning to you  jonathan brealy some really tough news  for people to be waking up to this  morning a price cap of 303  549 pounds announced by you this morning  a historic rise in energy prices people  might be asking  what's the point of off gem if it can't  protect its consumers  well look i talk to customers  all the time and i know that for many  many people this will be devastating  news this morning but let me explain  what's happening in the market and  what's driving the changes that we're  seeing  so after covid last year we saw lots of  economies grow particularly asia and  that already put pressure on the gas  market and as we saw prices began to  spike in around september of last year  now this year over and above that we  have seen a slow and deliberate cut-off  of gas by russia to the european market  and that has made prices spike much much  further when i look at the prices for  winter now for buying the gas they are  15 times what they normally are now that  would happen in petrol that would mean  it would cost us 400 to 500 pounds to  fill our car so ultimately the cost of  energy is changing dramatically and  that's why the price cap needs to change  and that's why we are announcing the  incredibly difficult news that it's  going up to  3549 pounds today  and yet if you look at the off chairman  website it does say that your role is to  protect consumers by working to deliver  a fairer energy system  but when you hear national energy action  predicting that 8.2 million people will  be in fuel poverty  from october people will feel that  consumers aren't being protected  well look what we are also saying today  is that the regulator has done  everything that it can the price cap was  designed principally to do one thing and  that was to stop those retail companies  charging unfair profits over and above  the cost of the energy they need to buy  and those profits are around zero in the  market today but what we can't do is  force companies to buy energy for less  than it costs because ultimately they  won't be able to afford to buy the  energy that we need what we're saying  today though is this is a big problem  this is a big problem for customers now  in 10 days time we will have a new  administration a new prime minister and  a new set of ministers and what we are  saying to them today is we will need to  work with you and the industry and ngos  and there will need to be urgent and  decisive action to tackle this problem  because the difficult news we have today  is that when i look out to winter and i  look out to next year there is continued  significant increasing price pressure so  we all need to work together but i am  confident that if we do so we will get  through this for customers but that will  mean urgent and decisive action from the  government  what should that urgent and decisive  action look like do you think  well we are working very very closely  with officials to make sure there are  big choices available to ministers and  the prime minister and the new  ministerial team now it's not my place  as a regulator to lay those plans out  that needs to be done rightly by elected  politicians but we are all working very  hard to make sure there are clear  choices available for the new team in 10  days time we will work with government  as we are doing today we will work with  the industry and ngos as we are doing  today but this does need further  intervention remember when prices  increased before government did act  government did put together a support  package what we are saying is given  these changes all of that support needs  needs to change and we do need  intervention that is urgent and decisive  in government to make sure that  customers are looked after this winter  we will play our part but a regulator  the industry and the ngos on their own  won't fix this we need to do this with  the government  do you think the system needs to change  is the energy price cap fit for purpose  anymore when it rises so steeply so  often  well look the price cap was designed to  do one thing and that was to make sure  that those retail companies aren't  charging what they used to charge they  used to charge up to six even nine  percent above the level that you're  seeing today imagine that added on top  of the bills that we are talking about  today what it can't do though is  subsidize the cost of energy that does  need to be paid for through the market  at the moment what we do need to do is  work with the government work with ngos  work with everyone to make sure that we  can tackle this i am confident that  customers will get through this this  winter but we do need to work together  urgently and decisively to make that  happen  there's a lot to do but i'm confident  that working together we can do this  apologies for interrupting i mean the  way that the price cap is working at the  moment  looks like it's protecting the companies  the energy companies at the expense of  the consumer  can you see how it's perceived to look  like perceived to be like that is that  how you say it  look if i can understand why everyone  today is feeling frustrated incredibly  worried and probably incredibly angry  but the price cap has to reflect the  legitimate cost in the market now when i  look at what's happening with the gas  market when i look frankly at what is  being caused by the russian shutdown of  gas to europe the costs are there they  are massively increasing and that does  need to be reflected in the price cap  but what we're not doing today which we  used to do five or six years ago is  adding big profits for the retail  companies on top of that so it is doing  its job but all of us need to work  together to do more to make sure  customers are protected and to make sure  ultimately economically and for  customers we get through this this  winter and beyond  but is the customer being put first here  given that  the way that the announcement is made it  used to be twice a year we're now going  to get it every three months there'll be  another announcement of another rise in  january just when people are going  through a winter and having to use more  energy that the consumer's needs are not  being thought through here are they  well we focus absolutely on consumer  needs but we all have to accept as we  saw last year when companies fail  ultimately those costs fall back on  consumers so when we make our decisions  we make them thinking only about what  cost falls on customers and how we best  look after customers now in this case  the price cap does need to change to  adapt to a more volatile market but  ultimately we need to work with  government because we accept that what  is there right now is not going to be  enough to deal with the situation we're  facing this winter and that's really why  we are saying there is a collective  effort here that needs to be made the  regulator cannot change this on our own  the industry cannot change on its own  the ngos cannot change it on their own  but with government working with the new  administration and the new ministerial  team we can get ahead of this and make  sure that customers are genuinely looked  after this winter it's really important  that we see what is coming and i accept  today's rise is a big shock for many  families and we work together to tackle  it that's why we're asking the  government the new government when it's  in place in 10 days time to act urgently  and decisively to make sure that we do  offset the impacts not just for  customers but for the economy as a whole  yesterday we saw a folk forecast for the  energy price cap  to rise by next april to over six  thousand pounds six thousand eight  hundred and three pounds was the  forecast is that the kind of reality we  have to face next year  well i would treat any  forecast for april with a huge amount of  caution i've seen the forecast even for  october although they were very accurate  a few weeks ago they were not accurate a  few months ago so i wouldn't hold on to  any particular figure but as i've said  when i look into the market  it is possible and i emphasize this it  is possible that  there is for example peace in russia and  ukraine this whole thing unwinds and  prices may stabilize or fall but it is  much much more likely that prices  continue to rise and what i wouldn't do  and what i caution anyone against doing  is saying what that figure would be for  next april it simply is incredible at  this stage but yes there is a big  challenge ahead of us this winter that's  why all the agencies that are involved  need to work together to act urgently  and decisively to make sure that we can  tackle this so today off-gem is saying  we need to work with others to tackle  this for customers  is it true that you're working on plans  to offer discounts to people who cut  down their energy usage at peak times  and if so how would that work  well look there was a great scheme run  by part of national grid and one of the  energy companies that does say if we  move our power so if you remember you  know the highest demand for power is  usually  early in the morning when we're waking  up we're having our breakfast and  it's sort of five or six at night when  many of us are cooking and many of us  are getting home now if we can shift  that power we can massively reduce the  needs of the system and what we are  saying with that scheme is that if you  do move your pack if you do move your  the time you use your energy  you should get some money back so i  think that's a great scheme that may  well help this winter but a great scheme  we should look to for the future because  the more we can change things like that  the more we will make the whole system  cost manageable and the whole system  costs come down and look the way the  country will get out of this situation  ultimately is to reduce our dependence  on the international gas market create  more homegrown energy and build on the  sorts of schemes that allow us allow us  to choose when we use our energy and  indeed get discounts if we're able to  move it away from those peaks the future  is really clear what we've got to do is  work to help customers through this  winter  okay well jonathan really  ceo of gem thanks very much indeed your  time this morning  thank you

